; The grue — Zork's darkness hazard.
;
; ZIL models this by rolling a random grue death whenever the player moves in an
; unlit room (gverbs.zil / gglobals.zil GRUE-FUNCTION). We drop the randomness so
; the world stays statically analyzable (frotz), and instead give a deterministic,
; configurable grace: the player may linger :grue-grace turns in the dark before
; the grue strikes.
;
; This is the same hazard-event shape as The Lurking Horror's `freezing` event
; (go outside -> freeze unless you get indoors; here: go dark -> get eaten unless
; you find light). It is queued from turn 1 via the world's :start-events, so the
; grue "is always lurking" whether or not the player has descended.
;
; The pitch-black warning itself is the engine's perception layer (the world
; :dark-message); this event only counts the dark turns and delivers the death.

(event grue-lurks
  :on-turn
    (cond
      ; In the light: safe. Reset the counter if it had started ticking.
      ((lit? (loc @player))
        (cond
          ((> (:dark-turns @player) 0)
            '((set @player :dark-turns 0) (success)))
          (true (success))))

      ; In the dark, still within the grace period: tick the counter.
      ((< (:dark-turns @player) (:grue-grace @player))
        (let ((?n (+ (:dark-turns @player) 1)))
          `((set @player :dark-turns ,?n) (success))))

      ; In the dark past the grace: the grue strikes.
      (true
        (blocked :reason death
                 :context ((death true)
                           (description "Oh, no! You have walked into the slavering fangs of a lurking grue!"))))))
